## M7 circuit-level results (rotated_memory_z, rounds = d)

Noise: Stim `after_clifford_depolarization` + data + measure + reset, all at the same `p`. Decoders: `aether_uf` vs built-in `pymatching`. Caps: `max_shots=1200`, `max_errors=40`.

| d | p | UF shots / errors / LER | MWPM shots / errors / LER |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 3 | 0.003 | 1200 / 9 / 0.0075 | 1200 / 7 / 0.0058 |
| 3 | 0.006 | 1200 / 32 / 0.027 | 1200 / 23 / 0.019 |
| 3 | 0.01 | 817 / 45 / 0.055 | 1200 / 78 / 0.065 |
| 3 | 0.015 | 369 / 45 / 0.122 | 1200 / 134 / 0.112 |
| 5 | 0.003 | 1200 / 9 / 0.0075 | 1200 / 6 / 0.0050 |
| 5 | 0.006 | 1200 / 36 / 0.030 | 1200 / 30 / 0.025 |
| 5 | 0.01 | 433 / 42 / 0.097 | 1200 / 102 / 0.085 |
| 5 | 0.015 | 157 / 40 / 0.255 | 273 / 57 / 0.209 |

**How to read this (do not over-claim a threshold):**

- UF tracks MWPM and is usually slightly worse, which is the expected heuristic gap.
- At `p ≥ 0.01`, **larger d is worse** for both decoders → this grid is mostly **above** the circuit-level threshold for this noise model (literature weighted UF ≈ 0.62%, MWPM ≈ 0.72% under a related but not identical circuit model).
- At `p = 0.003`, d=3 and d=5 UF LER are identical (9/1200). That is **not** distance suppression; shot noise. Need many more shots and `p` closer to 0.1%–0.5%.
- `max_errors=40` stopped UF early at high `p`, so those LERs are **not** fair head-to-heads (different shot counts). Next sweeps should raise `max_errors` or disable it for comparison rows.

## M9 dense sweep (matched 800 shots unless noted)

Circuit-level, `p ∈ {0.002, 0.004, 0.006}`, rounds = d, 800 shots both decoders. Source: `results/threshold_dense.csv`. Runtime ~12.5 min, 2 workers.

| d | p | UF LER | MWPM LER |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 3 | 0.002 | 0.00375 (3/800) | 0.005 (4/800) |
| 5 | 0.002 | 0 (0/800) | 0 (0/800) |
| 7 | 0.002 | 0.00125 (1/800) | 0 (0/800) |
| 3 | 0.004 | 0.00875 | 0.015 |
| 5 | 0.004 | 0.010 | 0.00625 |
| 7 | 0.004 | 0.0075 | 0.00625 |
| 3 | 0.006 | 0.0225 | 0.030 |
| 5 | 0.006 | 0.015 | 0.021 |
| 7 | 0.006 | 0.0225 | 0.0125 |

At `p=0.002`, d=5 and d=7 beat d=3 — first hint of circuit-level suppression. Not enough errors to quote a threshold. At `p=0.006`, d=7 UF is worse than MWPM (0.0225 vs 0.0125).

Phenomenological **same p grid** (too far below the ~2.6% literature threshold): MWPM is ~0 at d=7; UF is not (e.g. d=7 p=0.006: UF 19/800 vs MWPM 0/800). Confirmed off-Sinter: d=7 p=0.006, 200 shots, UF 5 vs MWPM 0.

Phenom nearer threshold (`results/threshold_phenom_near_thr.csv`, 400 shots):

| d | p | UF LER | MWPM LER |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 3 | 0.015 | 0.0425 | 0.0125 |
| 5 | 0.015 | 0.055 | 0.005 |
| 3 | 0.02 | 0.0525 | 0.0425 |
| 5 | 0.02 | 0.0725 | 0.025 |
| 3 | 0.025 | 0.0725 | 0.040 |
| 5 | 0.025 | 0.13 | 0.0275 |

MWPM **suppresses** with d at these p; UF **anti-suppresses** in this **pre-M10** table. Root cause: peeling skipped every boundary leaf, so a cluster that touched two virtual boundaries (common for equal-weight measurement edges) never peeled the detector. **Do not use these phenom LERs.** Post-fix numbers are under M10.

Reproduce:

```bash
python3 scripts/threshold_sweep.py --noise both --distances 3,5,7 --ps 0.002,0.004,0.006 \
  --max-shots 800 --max-errors 1000000000 --workers 2 --out results/threshold_dense.csv
python3 scripts/threshold_sweep.py --noise phenom --distances 3,5 --ps 0.015,0.02,0.025 \
  --max-shots 400 --max-errors 1000000000 --workers 2 --out results/threshold_phenom_near_thr.csv
```

